A||N||1||rockcrawl||rear swaybar, cheap & easy!||||14||
Z||000000||rockcrawl||11-20-2000||06:44 PM||||I decided to put a stock front swaybar on the rear of my '87 GT yesterday. What a cheap and easy upgrade! It took me about an hour to complete, and cost me nothing. I had a swaybar from a low-mileage parts car and I re-used all the rubber bushings. I replaced the end link bolts with 5/16 x 3.5" bolts and left out the sleeves, works great. The car handles quite differently, it will take a while to get used to.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000001||webbee||11-20-2000||07:09 PM||||Get the polyurethane links. It will stiffen it up a bit. <P>I think sway bars are one of the best bang for the buck add ons you can do to a car. It sure makes cornering fun.||||reg||1
